; expect 42
; A closure held as an `Int` and then applied. `cast` is the documented
; way to move a function value through a plain `Int` - the
; uniform-representation rule makes it an ordinary thing to write - and
; applying the result is the only reason to do it.
;
; `walkAppChain` flattens a whole application spine into one argument
; accumulator before dispatching on its head, so `((cast Int f) 41)`
; reaches the `cast` branch as `[Int, f, 41]`, not `[Int, f]`. That
; branch emitted the LAST argument, which is right only when there is no
; surplus: it emitted `41` and returned it, `check`-clean, at exit 0,
; for a one-parameter `f`. `cast` now consumes its two arguments and
; applies anything beyond them to its result.
;
;   (via1 41)   42 - one surplus argument, a one-parameter closure.
;                    Under the old rule this was 41: the argument
;                    itself, returned as the answer.
;   (via2 20)   42 - two surplus arguments through a two-parameter
;                    lambda, so the surplus applies one at a time and
;                    the intermediate value is the inner closure. Under
;                    the old rule this was 22.
;   (plain)     42 - a `cast` with NO surplus still answers its second
;                    argument, which is what every existing use needs.
(:: via1 (-> Int Int))
(fn (via1 n)
  (let ((f (lambda (x) (+ x 1))))
    ((cast Int f) n)))

(:: via2 (-> Int Int))
(fn (via2 n)
  (let ((g (lambda (x y) (+ x y))))
    ((cast Int g) n 22)))

(:: plain Int)
(fn (plain) (cast Int 42))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (if (&& (== (via1 41) 42)
          (&& (== (via2 20) 42) (== (plain) 42)))
      42
      1))
